===Etymology 1===
{{PIE root|en|ped}}
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|fecchen}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|feċċan}}, {{m|ang|fæċċan||to fetch}}. In one view, an alteration of {{m|ang|fetian}}, {{m|ang|fatian}} {{gloss|"to fetch, marry"; whence also English {{m|en|fet}}}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*fatōną}}, {{m|gem-pro|*fatjaną||to fetch}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*ped-||foot}}.
{{rel-top|cognates}}
Cognate with West Frisian {{m|fy|fetsje||to grasp}}, Dutch {{m|nl|vatten||to catch, grasp, understand}}, German {{m|de|fassen||to grasp, touch}}, {{cog|fo|fata||to grasp, understand}}, {{cog|is|feta||to go, step}}. See {{l|en|foot}}. In another view, allied to {{cog|ang|facian}}, {{m|ang|fācian||to acquire, get, obtain; try to obtain, wish for, desire; get to, reach}}, related to {{cog|ang|fǣċan||to wish to go}}, {{cog|is|fíkast||to desire, covet, crave}}, and perhaps to {{cog|ofs|faka||to prepare}}.
{{rel-bottom}}

